/* Track: Noticing the disagreement. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * Everything downstream assumes you know you disagree. Most of the time you do
 * not, or you know it in a form too vague to say, and by the time it has words
 * the decision has already been taken. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "noticing-silence-is-the-default",
  track: "noticing", level: "everyday",
  title: "Silence is the normal state, so your own silence tells you nothing about the risk",
  source: "Milliken, Morrison and Hewlin, An Exploratory Study of Employee Silence (Journal of Management Studies, 2003)",
  gateIntro: "A decision was made in a meeting you were in last Tuesday. You had a reservation at the time. You did not say it, and afterwards you decided the reservation had probably not been important enough to raise. Since then two colleagues have separately mentioned, in passing and without pushing it, that they were not sure about that decision either. Nobody has put anything in the channel. The work has started.",
  gatePrompt: "You are going to reopen a decision that everyone has moved on from, a week late. Write the first sentence.",
  evidence: "Forty employees across industries, interviewed about issues they had chosen not to raise with a superior. Eighty five per cent reported at least one such issue. The most common reasons given were fear of being labelled negatively and a belief that speaking would change nothing. An interview study with no control group and no behavioural measure, so it establishes prevalence in a sample and nothing about cause.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Withholding is what almost everyone does almost always, so the fact that you said nothing carries no information about whether the concern deserved airing.",
  why: "People treat their own reticence as a signal. The reasoning runs: I did not speak, and I am a reasonable person who speaks when it matters, therefore it did not matter. That inference would work if speaking were the default and silence the exception. It is the other way round. In the Milliken interviews the withheld issues were not trivia; they included concerns about the competence of colleagues, about pay, and about processes people believed were harming the business. The employees who withheld them were not indifferent. They had run a calculation whose inputs were the cost of being seen as a complainer and the low expected chance of anything changing, and neither of those inputs is about whether the concern was correct.\n\nSo the useful move is to stop consulting your behaviour and consult the concern. Written down, would it survive being read back to you by somebody neutral? That question has an answer. Did I feel strongly enough to speak at the time does not, because the feeling was competing with a social cost that has nothing to do with the merits.",
  failureMode: "A month later the thing you did not raise shows up in a review, and three people say some version of I wondered about that too. Nobody was lying and nobody was cowardly. Each of them read their own silence the way you read yours, and the collective read of nine private doubts was unanimous support.",
  worked: "A week late is not too late, and pretending otherwise is what makes it permanently too late. Open by conceding the timing rather than hiding it, because the reader has already noticed:\n\n\"I want to reopen the sequencing decision from last Tuesday. I had a reservation in the room and did not raise it, which is on me, and it has not gone away.\"\n\nThen the concern, in one sentence, with no preamble about how everyone is probably right:\n\n\"If we ship the migration before the read path is behind a flag, we have no way to roll back on a bad Friday.\"\n\nNote what is missing. No apology beyond the one sentence, no I might be missing something, no I am sure you have already thought about this. Those are all bids to be excused, and each one gives the room permission to move on.",
  experiment: "Take the last three decisions you were in the room for and had a reservation about. Write each reservation down as one sentence now, with the meeting long over and the social cost gone. Read the three back. The question is not whether you should have spoken; it is how many of the three still look substantive once nobody is watching. If it is more than one, your filter is calibrated to the social cost rather than to the concern.",
  reflection: "Which reservation from the last month have you quietly reclassified as not important enough, and does it survive being written in one sentence?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is your own decision not to speak up a poor guide to whether the concern was worth raising?",
    a: "Because withholding is the base rate rather than the exception. In interviews, eighty five per cent of employees named at least one issue they had not raised, and the reasons they gave were social cost and expected futility, neither of which is about whether the concern was correct. Reading your silence as evidence assumes speaking is the default, which it is not."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are three reservations I did not raise. Help me work out which of them still holds up on the merits, stripped of the reason I stayed quiet."
},
{
  id: "noticing-body-before-argument",
  track: "noticing", level: "everyday",
  title: "The objection arrives as a physical state several minutes before it arrives as a sentence",
  source: "Crew resource management training practice, and the assertive-statement drill used in airline and healthcare curricula",
  gateIntro: "Twenty minutes into a design review your shoulders are up and you have stopped taking notes. You have read the same line of the document three times. Nothing anybody has said is wrong exactly, and if someone asked you right now what your problem was you would not have an answer ready. The presenter is about to move to the next slide and ask whether there are any concerns.",
  gatePrompt: "You have the feeling but not yet the argument, and the window is closing. Write the sentence that buys you the time to find it.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is standard practice in crew resource management curricula, where trainees are taught to treat unease as reportable data and to voice it before it is fully formed, and in the equivalent healthcare programmes derived from them. The rationale is operational rather than experimental: an incompletely formed objection stated early is recoverable, and a fully formed one stated after the decision is not.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Say the unease before you can justify it: \"Something about this is bothering me and I have not worked out what. Give me a minute.\"",
  idea: "Treat the physical signal as reportable in itself, and say it out loud before you can defend it, because the alternative is to stay quiet until the argument is ready and the argument is never ready in time.",
  why: "There is a trap built into being conscientious. You will not speak until you have something rigorous, and assembling something rigorous takes minutes you do not have, so the meeting closes and you leave with an objection that finishes forming in the corridor. This is not a failure of courage. It is a sequencing error, and it is completely avoidable, because there is a legitimate move available at the moment you have only the feeling: report the feeling.\n\nAviation training makes this explicit and unembarrassing. The unease of an experienced operator is treated as an instrument reading, not as an emotion to be managed privately. That framing is what makes it sayable. You are not asking the room to accept your intuition as a conclusion. You are logging an observation and requesting the time to interpret it, and both of those are ordinary professional requests that nobody can reasonably refuse.",
  failureMode: "You wait until you can articulate it. The meeting ends. The articulation shows up at nine that evening, complete and correct, and by then the only channel available is a message that reads as second-guessing a decision everyone has already left the room believing in.",
  worked: "The whole move is one sentence and a request for time:\n\n\"Something here is bothering me and I have not pinned down what it is. Can we hold on this slide for a minute?\"\n\nIf the room will not stop, take the smaller version, which costs nothing and preserves the option:\n\n\"I want to flag that I am not comfortable and I cannot say why yet. I will write it up by end of day. Please do not treat this as agreement.\"\n\nThat last clause is the load-bearing one. Without it, silence after the flag gets read as the concern having evaporated. With it, you have converted an unformed feeling into a placeholder that the record now carries.",
  experiment: "Pick the next meeting where a decision gets made and pay attention to your posture rather than the content. Note the timestamp when your shoulders come up, and the timestamp when you could have stated the objection in a sentence. In most people the gap runs to several minutes and the decision lands inside it. That gap is the thing this move is for.",
  reflection: "When did you last leave a meeting and finish forming your objection in the corridor, and what would the placeholder sentence have cost you in the room?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does waiting until you can articulate an objection reliably lose the argument, and what is the legitimate move available before the argument exists?",
    a: "Because assembling a defensible objection takes minutes the meeting does not give you, so the decision lands inside the gap and the finished argument arrives afterwards as second-guessing. The available move is to report the unease itself as an observation and request time, explicitly stating that silence should not be read as agreement."
  },
  deepDive: "I have a persistent unease about a design I cannot yet articulate. Interrogate me until the objection has a shape, then give me the sentence."
},
{
  id: "noticing-implicit-voice-theories",
  track: "noticing", level: "costly",
  title: "You are obeying rules about speaking that you have never once examined",
  source: "Detert and Edmondson, Implicit Voice Theories (Academy of Management Journal, 2011)",
  gateIntro: "You have found a real problem in a plan your skip level presented at an all hands. Your own manager was not in the loop on it. The obvious route is to reply on the thread where the plan was posted, which the skip level reads and roughly two hundred other people read as well. You notice you are looking for a way to have the conversation somewhere else, and you have not asked yourself why.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence as you would post it publicly on that thread, not the version you would send in a private message.",
  evidence: "A programme of qualitative and survey work in a technology firm identifying taken-for-granted beliefs about speaking to authority, held widely and acquired early. Examples the authors recovered include do not embarrass the boss in public, do not go over your boss's head, and you need solid data before you speak. Employees invoked these as self-evident rather than as calculations. Single-organisation studies, self-report throughout, and no test of whether acting against the beliefs actually carries the consequences they predict.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "The reason you did not speak is usually a rule you absorbed before you arrived here, not a risk assessment you ran about this room, and the rule is available for inspection as soon as you ask what it is.",
  why: "Detert and Edmondson's finding is that the constraint operates before deliberation rather than during it. People do not weigh the odds of retaliation and decide against speaking. They experience the option as unavailable, in the way that a rule of etiquette is unavailable, and when pressed they justify it with something that sounds like received wisdom rather than evidence. Do not embarrass the boss in public is the clearest example. It is stated as though it were a law of organisations. It was acquired somewhere, often in a previous job, often from watching one incident involving somebody else.\n\nWhat makes this actionable is that unexamined rules do not survive examination well. Asked directly whether this specific manager, in this specific forum, actually punishes public correction, most people find they have no instance to point to. Some do, and for them the rule is calibrated and should be kept. The point is not that the rules are always wrong. It is that you are currently unable to tell which of yours are.",
  failureMode: "You route the concern into a private message to preserve someone's dignity in public. The public thread now contains two hundred people who saw the plan and no correction, so the plan proceeds by default while the correction sits in a direct message that its recipient can act on, defer, or quietly not act on with nobody the wiser.",
  worked: "Public correction of a senior person is survivable when it corrects the artefact rather than the person. Address the plan, use the first person for the claim, and leave the author out of the sentence entirely:\n\n\"One thing on the migration plan: step three assumes the read replicas are caught up, and on our current lag they are usually about forty minutes behind at that time of day. If that is right, the cutover window in the plan is too short. Happy to be wrong on the lag number.\"\n\nWhat makes that publishable is that no clause is about the author. Compare the version most people write, which is about the author throughout: \"I think you may have missed something in step three.\" Same content, and now there is a person in the sentence who has to be defended.",
  experiment: "Write down the rule you are currently obeying, in the imperative, as though it were printed in a handbook. Then look for the instance. Who here was actually punished for breaking it, when, and did you see it or hear about it? Most of the time you will find the rule arrived from a previous employer or from a story told second hand, and it is being enforced against you by nobody but you.",
  reflection: "Which of your rules about speaking to authority came from watching one incident, and has this organisation ever actually enforced it?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is an implicit voice theory, and why is naming one an effective intervention?",
    a: "A taken-for-granted belief about speaking to authority, acquired early and held as self-evident rather than derived from evidence, such as do not embarrass the boss in public. Naming it works because it operates before deliberation, so it has never been examined, and most people find on inspection that they have no local instance of it being enforced."
  },
  deepDive: "Interrogate the rules I am obeying about who I can contradict and where. For each one, ask me for the specific instance I saw it enforced."
},
{
  id: "noticing-left-hand-column",
  track: "noticing", level: "costly",
  title: "The argument you are actually having is the one you are not saying out loud",
  source: "Chris Argyris, on defensive routines and the left-hand column exercise (Overcoming Organizational Defenses, 1990)",
  gateIntro: "A colleague has proposed rebuilding a service you own. In the thread you have written that it is an interesting idea worth exploring and asked two clarifying questions about timelines. What you have been thinking through the whole exchange is that this is a resume-driven rewrite of something that works, and that the two clarifying questions were chosen because they are the ones you expect no good answer to. The thread is waiting on you.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence that says the thing you have actually been thinking, in a form you could send.",
  evidence: "No trial. Argyris built this from decades of recorded consulting interventions and taught it as a diagnostic exercise: write what was said in the right column, what you were thinking and did not say in the left, and read the gap. Widely used in executive education and never tested experimentally. Treat it as an instrument for seeing your own behaviour rather than as an established finding.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Write the conversation in two columns, what you said and what you were thinking, and the width of the gap is a direct measurement of how much of the disagreement is currently unavailable to the person you are disagreeing with.",
  why: "The exercise works because it makes an asymmetry visible that is otherwise invisible from the inside. You experience the exchange as containing your real position, because your real position is present to you the whole time. Your counterpart experiences an exchange containing two clarifying questions about timelines. Both of you then leave believing you have communicated, and only one of you is right.\n\nWhat the left-hand column usually reveals is that the unsaid material is not unspeakable. It is unflattering, which is a different thing. Resume-driven rewrite is a genuinely rude formulation, but the claim underneath it is a normal engineering claim about whether the expected benefit clears the cost, and that version is entirely sayable. The work is not suppressing the left column or blurting it. It is translating it, and you cannot translate what you have not written down.",
  failureMode: "You ask the two questions you expect no good answer to, your colleague answers them adequately because they were narrow questions, and the rewrite is now approved on the record with your clarifying questions resolved. Your actual objection was never on the table and therefore was never rebutted, which will not stop it from being treated as having been.",
  worked: "The translation runs from the unflattering version to the falsifiable one. Left column: this is a resume-driven rewrite. What is sayable, and is the same claim:\n\n\"I want to put the direct version of my concern on the record rather than keep asking around it. I do not think the benefit clears the cost here. The service has had two incidents in eighteen months and both were config, not architecture. What would you expect to get from the rewrite that we could not get from the config work?\"\n\nThe last sentence is what stops this being an accusation. You have stated the objection plainly and then handed over the specific thing that would change your mind. Somebody who does have a good answer can now give it, and somebody who does not has to say so.",
  experiment: "Take a thread you are currently in where you have been asking questions rather than stating a position. Write the two columns out for real, actual sentences in both. Then take the strongest item in the left column and write the sayable version of it. The test of the sayable version is that it contains a claim your counterpart could rebut with evidence.",
  reflection: "In your most active current disagreement, what is in your left-hand column, and what is the falsifiable version of it?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does the left-hand column exercise measure, and what does it usually turn out that the unsaid material needs?",
    a: "The gap between what you said and what you were thinking, which measures how much of your disagreement is unavailable to the other person. The unsaid material is usually not unspeakable but unflattering, and it needs translating into a claim that names the cost or the risk and states what would change your mind."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is my left-hand column on a current disagreement. Translate each item into a claim the other person could actually rebut."
},
{
  id: "noticing-normalised-deviance",
  track: "noticing", level: "line",
  title: "You cannot object to the thing you have stopped seeing, and you stop seeing it by surviving it",
  source: "Diane Vaughan, The Challenger Launch Decision (1996)",
  gateIntro: "Your team has deployed on Friday afternoons for two years. It was flagged as a risk once, early on, and nothing bad happened, so the practice continued. A new engineer joined six weeks ago and asked about it in their first week, and someone explained the history and they have not raised it since. This week's change touches billing. Nobody in the planning thread has mentioned the day of the week.",
  evidence: "A sociological reconstruction of the O-ring decision history at NASA from primary documents and interviews, showing that each acceptance of out-of-family erosion became the baseline against which the next was judged, without any single decision being reckless. One deep historical case, enormously influential as a lens, never subjected to any test that could have falsified it. The mechanism is plausible and the evidence for it is one accident.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "A risk that has been survived repeatedly stops registering as a risk at all, so the strongest signal available to you is the reaction of somebody who has not yet survived it.",
  why: "Vaughan's account displaced the convenient story about Challenger, which was that managers overrode engineers under schedule pressure. What the documents showed was slower and worse: erosion outside the expected range was observed, analysed, and accepted, and the acceptance then defined what counted as expected for the next flight. Nobody in that sequence made a decision they would have recognised as gambling. Each step was small relative to the step before it, which is precisely the property that makes the drift invisible from the inside.\n\nThe practical consequence is that introspection cannot find this. You are asking a judgement to audit the process that formed it. What can find it is the person whose baseline was set somewhere else, which is why a new hire's first two weeks are an expiring asset. They will ask about the Friday deploys, someone will explain the history, and the explanation will recalibrate them. After that they are as blind as everyone else and the window has closed.",
  failureMode: "The new engineer asks, gets the history, and concludes the team has thought about it. In fact nobody has thought about it since the original decision, which was made when the service did not touch billing. The question was the last audit the practice was going to get, and it was answered by reassuring the auditor.",
  worked: "Two moves. First, harvest the question instead of answering it, at the moment it is asked:\n\n\"Hold on. Before I give you the history, say why it looked wrong to you. You are the only person here who can still see it.\"\n\nSecond, if the window has already closed, reopen it against the change in conditions rather than against the practice, because the practice has two years of survival behind it and will win that argument:\n\n\"We adopted Friday deploys when the service could not lose money. This change touches billing. I do not think the original call covers the current risk and I want it re-decided rather than inherited.\"",
  experiment: "Find the most recent person to join your team and ask them, explicitly, what struck them as odd in their first fortnight that they have since stopped noticing. Write the list down without defending any item on it. Then, for each one, find the decision that made it acceptable and check whether the conditions of that decision still hold.",
  reflection: "What practice on your team was accepted under conditions that no longer hold, and who was the last person to ask about it?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does normalisation of deviance resist introspection, and what is the practical detector for it?",
    a: "Because each acceptance of an out-of-range observation resets the baseline for the next, so no single step looks like a gamble and the judgement doing the auditing is the one that drifted. The detector is someone whose baseline was set elsewhere, typically a new joiner in their first weeks, and their questions have to be harvested before they are answered away."
  },
  deepDive: "Help me list the practices on my team that were accepted under conditions that have since changed, and which of them nobody has questioned in over a year."
},
{
  id: "noticing-pluralistic-ignorance",
  track: "noticing", level: "costly",
  title: "Everyone in the room privately disagrees and each of them thinks they are the only one",
  source: "Prentice and Miller, Pluralistic Ignorance and Alcohol Use on Campus (Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 1993)",
  gateIntro: "The reorganisation was announced three weeks ago. In the all hands nobody objected. Since then you have had four one-to-one conversations, each of which arrived at some version of well, we will see, accompanied by a facial expression that was not neutral. In none of those four conversations did either person say the plan was bad. There is a leadership question and answer session on Thursday.",
  gatePrompt: "You are going to be the first person to say it in public. Write the question or statement exactly as you would ask it on Thursday.",
  evidence: "Students consistently estimated their peers were more comfortable with campus drinking norms than they themselves were, with the gap persisting over time and predicting behaviour. The pattern has since been reproduced across many domains including workplace norms and classroom participation, and the underlying inference error is well established. What is less well established is the size of the effect in any particular organisation, which nobody has measured for yours.",
  replication: "replicated",
  idea: "When everyone privately doubts and nobody says so, each person reads the public silence as evidence of everyone else's genuine support, and one person stating their doubt plainly is usually enough to collapse the illusion.",
  why: "The mechanism is an asymmetry of access. You know your own hedging is a hedge, because you were there when you decided to hedge. You do not have that access to anybody else's, so you read their we will see as mild agreement while knowing yours meant something closer to this is a mistake. Everybody does this simultaneously, and the aggregate is a room where the public consensus is unanimous and the private distribution is not.\n\nThe reason this is worth knowing is that it changes what you think you are doing when you speak. If you believe you are the lone objector, speaking is a high-cost minority act. If you suspect pluralistic ignorance, speaking is closer to a public good with a low expected cost, because the four people who hedged to you privately are sitting in the room and none of them can move first either. The evidence for the general phenomenon is strong. The evidence that it is operating in your particular room is the four hedges, which is decent circumstantial evidence and not proof.",
  failureMode: "You conclude from the silence in the all hands that you are an outlier and moderate your position accordingly. So do the four people who hedged to you. The organisation proceeds having conducted a genuine consultation in which nobody lied and the result was the opposite of the private distribution.",
  worked: "Do not speak for the others, because you cannot and it will be denied. Speak for yourself and describe the pattern without attributing positions:\n\n\"I want to say publicly what I have been saying in one-to-ones, because I think a few of us have been having this conversation privately and not here. I do not think the new reporting lines fix the coordination problem, and I think they cost us the on-call rotation that currently works. I would rather be argued with in the open than agree with it quietly.\"\n\nThe last sentence does two jobs. It signals that you are not looking for a fight, and it gives everyone else in the room a socially cheap way to follow, which is the entire mechanism you are trying to trigger.",
  experiment: "After the next contentious decision, count your private conversations for two weeks and score each one: explicit support, explicit objection, or a hedge. Then compare that distribution with the public record. If hedges dominate privately and the public record is unanimous, you have the signature, and you now know roughly how many people are waiting for somebody to move first.",
  reflection: "What are you currently hedging about privately and not saying publicly, and how many hedges have you heard back from others on the same thing?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is pluralistic ignorance, and why does one person speaking plainly tend to collapse it?",
    a: "A state where most people privately disagree but each reads the public silence as everyone else's genuine support, because you have access to your own hedging as a hedge and no access to anybody else's. One plain statement collapses it by supplying the public evidence that private dissent exists, which makes following socially cheap for everyone who could not move first."
  },
  deepDive: "I think a decision on my team has unanimous public support and mixed private support. Help me work out how to test that without putting anyone on the spot."
},
{
  id: "noticing-information-cascade",
  track: "noticing", level: "costly",
  title: "It can be individually rational to discard your own evidence, and the room ends up with none",
  source: "Bikhchandani, Hirshleifer and Welch (Journal of Political Economy, 1992), tested experimentally by Anderson and Holt (American Economic Review, 1997)",
  gateIntro: "Three people have now spoken in favour of the vendor, in order of seniority, and each cited the same integration argument. You have run the integration yourself on a previous team and it took two quarters rather than the six weeks in the deck. You are fourth to speak. You notice you are wondering whether your experience was unusual, and you have started assembling a version of your point that could be read as broadly supportive.",
  evidence: "A model in which agents observing predecessors' choices rationally ignore their own private signal once the public tally is strong enough, so information stops entering the pool. Anderson and Holt reproduced the predicted cascades in a laboratory setting with monetary incentives, and the result has been replicated across many subsequent experiments. The lab evidence is solid. The extrapolation to a specific meeting with status differences and no payoff structure is an extrapolation.",
  replication: "replicated",
  idea: "Once several people have publicly agreed, the rational move for each new speaker is to follow the tally rather than their own evidence, so the way to break it is to contribute the evidence itself rather than a contrary conclusion.",
  why: "The uncomfortable part of the cascade result is that nobody has to be a coward or a fool. Each speaker is updating correctly on the information available, which by then is three public agreements. Their own private signal is one observation against three, so discarding it is defensible. The failure is structural: because they discard it rather than state it, the pool never gets any richer, and the fourth speaker faces the same distorted evidence base as the third.\n\nThis has a precise implication for how you speak. A contrary vote adds one to a tally and is easily outweighed. A contrary observation adds information that was not in the room and cannot be outweighed by counting, because it is not the same kind of object. Two quarters, on this integration, on a team I was on is not an opinion about the vendor. It is data, and once it is public the next speaker's calculation genuinely changes.",
  failureMode: "You soften your two quarters into a general note that integrations often run long, which is a conclusion rather than an observation and is immediately absorbed by the presenter agreeing that yes, they have built in buffer. Your actual number never enters the room, and the deck's six weeks survives unchallenged.",
  worked: "Lead with the observation, hold the conclusion back, and make the number specific enough that it cannot be absorbed:\n\n\"Before we go further I want to put a data point in. I ran this integration at my last company, same product, and it took us just over two quarters end to end. I am not saying six weeks is impossible here, I am saying our estimate and my experience are a factor of four apart and I would like to understand why before we commit.\"\n\nThe factor of four is what does the work. Integrations often run long invites reassurance. A factor of four demands a reconciliation, and the person who has to produce it is the person who wrote the six.",
  experiment: "In the next meeting where several people agree in sequence before you speak, write down your private signal before you open your mouth, in the form of a specific observation with a number or a date attached. Then say the observation and stop, without stating what you think should be done. Notice whether the discussion changes shape, which it usually does, because you have moved the room off counting and onto reconciling.",
  reflection: "When did you last discard a specific observation of your own because several people had already agreed, and what was the number you did not say?",
  recall: {
    q: "In an information cascade, what exactly goes wrong, and why is a contrary observation more effective than a contrary conclusion?",
    a: "Each speaker rationally follows the public tally instead of their private signal, so no new information enters the pool and later speakers face the same distorted evidence as earlier ones. A contrary conclusion just adds one to a tally and can be outvoted. A contrary observation adds information that was not present, which changes the calculation of everyone who speaks after it."
  },
  deepDive: "I have a specific observation that contradicts the consensus in an upcoming decision. Help me strip it down to the data and take my conclusion out of it."
},
{
  id: "noticing-preference-versus-risk",
  track: "noticing", level: "everyday",
  title: "Sort your objection into taste, cost or harm before you spend anything on it",
  source: "Stop-work authority as practised in nuclear, aviation and heavy industry, where the threshold for halting work is defined in advance",
  gateIntro: "You disagree with three things in the proposal in front of you. The naming convention is not the one you would have picked. The rollout order means the smallest tenant gets the change last, which will make support harder for a fortnight. And the migration has no tested rollback, which means a bad deploy is unrecoverable during business hours. You have roughly two minutes of goodwill in this meeting.",
  gatePrompt: "You can raise one. Write the sentence for the one you pick, and make the reason you picked it audible in the sentence.",
  evidence: "No trial. High-hazard industries define in advance which conditions justify stopping work, precisely so that the judgement is not made under pressure by the person who has to bear the social cost of stopping it. The design principle is that categories must be set when nobody is in the room. There is no experimental evidence that individuals who self-categorise this way speak more effectively, only a long operational tradition of organisations that categorise centrally.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Sort every objection into taste, cost or harm before raising it, because they warrant completely different amounts of force and the most common error is spending a harm-level intervention on a taste-level disagreement.",
  why: "Your credibility in a room is a budget, and it is drawn down by the intensity of your interventions rather than by their number. Someone who pushes hard on the naming convention has spent the budget before reaching the rollback, and by the time they reach it the room has already classified them as a person who pushes hard on everything. That classification is the real cost, because it is applied retrospectively to the important objection too.\n\nThe three categories are not equally hard to tell apart. Taste and cost blur, and people flatter their taste by describing it as cost. The reliable test is whether you can name the consequence and who bears it. The naming convention has no consequence you can name. The rollout order has one, borne by support, measurable in a fortnight of extra tickets. The missing rollback has one borne by every customer during an unrecoverable window. Those are three different objects and only the third is worth stopping the room for.",
  failureMode: "You raise all three because you have them all, in the order they appear in the document, which puts the naming convention first. By the time you reach the rollback you have used your two minutes and the room's attention, and the rollback gets thirty seconds and a we will look at it.",
  worked: "Raise the harm, name the sort explicitly, and give the other two away in public so the room knows what you did:\n\n\"I have three comments and I am only going to make one, because the other two are preferences and this one is not. There is no tested rollback on the migration. If a deploy goes bad at eleven on a Tuesday we have no way back and the outage runs until we fix forward. I would like that closed before this ships. My other two are about naming and rollout order and I will leave them in the doc as comments.\"\n\nAnnouncing the triage is not throat-clearing. It tells the room your intensity is calibrated, which is what makes the intensity credible when you use it.",
  experiment: "Take a document you have already reviewed and go back through your comments, labelling each one taste, cost or harm. Force the label by writing the consequence and the bearer next to each. Most reviewers find the majority are taste dressed as cost, and that the one genuine harm was raised in the same register as everything else.",
  reflection: "In your last review, how many comments could you name a consequence and a bearer for, and did the one that mattered get more force than the others?",
  recall: {
    q: "What are the three sorts an objection falls into, what test separates them, and why does the sorting matter?",
    a: "Taste, cost and harm. The test is whether you can name a specific consequence and who bears it: taste has none, cost has a bounded one, harm has a serious or unrecoverable one. Sorting matters because credibility is drawn down by intensity rather than frequency, so force spent on taste is unavailable for harm and marks you as someone who pushes hard on everything."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are my objections to a plan. Sort them into taste, cost and harm, and force me to name the consequence and the bearer for each."
},
{
  id: "noticing-question-you-swallowed",
  track: "noticing", level: "everyday",
  title: "The clarifying question you decided not to ask is the disagreement before it has grown up",
  source: "Edgar Schein, Humble Inquiry (2013)",
  gateIntro: "Halfway through the architecture walkthrough somebody says the queue will absorb the burst. You almost ask how big the burst is assumed to be, and then do not, because the presenter has clearly thought about this and the question would sound like you were not following. Forty minutes later the design is approved. You still do not know what number they had in mind, and neither, it turns out, does anyone else in the room.",
  gatePrompt: "The moment has passed and the design is approved. Write the sentence that reopens it, starting from the question you swallowed.",
  evidence: "No trial. Schein's argument is drawn from decades of process consultation and case observation, and holds that asking questions to which you genuinely do not know the answer is both undervalued and status-costly in hierarchical settings. It has been enormously influential in organisational development and has never been tested in a way that could have shown it wrong.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Ask the number. \"What burst size is that assuming?\" is cheaper than any objection and often ends the argument before it starts.",
  idea: "Track the questions you decide not to ask, because a swallowed clarifying question is almost always a disagreement in an early enough form to be cheap, and it is the only form in which you can raise it without a position to defend.",
  why: "A question costs less than an objection in every dimension that matters. It does not commit you to a conclusion you might have to retreat from. It cannot be dismissed as negativity. It does not require you to have done the work of building an alternative. And it frequently resolves the matter outright, because a meaningful share of apparent disagreements turn out to be the two parties holding different unstated numbers.\n\nWhat stops people asking is a status calculation that has the sign wrong. The fear is that the question exposes you as not following. In practice, the specific question with a number in it exposes the opposite, and the room's usual reaction to what burst size is that assuming is not that you were not following but that nobody knew. The reason to track the swallowed ones deliberately is that you will not remember them otherwise. They do not feel like events. They feel like nothing happening.",
  failureMode: "The design ships with an unstated burst assumption. Nine months later the burst is four times that, the queue does not absorb it, and the incident review concludes the design assumed a smaller burst. Everyone in the walkthrough is surprised, because nobody in the walkthrough ever heard a number.",
  worked: "Reopening after approval works better as a request for the record than as a challenge, because it gives the author somewhere to go that is not defence:\n\n\"One thing I should have asked in the walkthrough and did not. When we say the queue absorbs the burst, what burst size is that assuming? I want it written into the doc rather than in our heads, because I do not think we all have the same number.\"\n\nIf the answer turns out to be that there is no number, you have converted a swallowed question into a design gap without ever having had to make an accusation, and the person who now has to find the number is the person who wrote the claim.",
  experiment: "For one week keep a note in your phone and add a line every time you decide not to ask something in a meeting. Do not act on any of them yet, just collect. At the end of the week read the list and mark the ones that were actually disagreements. Most people find between a third and a half were, and that the reason for swallowing every one of them was identical.",
  reflection: "What is the last question you did not ask because it might have sounded like you were not following, and what would asking it have cost?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is a swallowed clarifying question worth tracking, and what advantage does the question form have over the objection form?",
    a: "Because a large share of them are disagreements in an early, cheap form, and they leave no trace in memory since not asking feels like nothing happening. The question form commits you to no position you would have to retreat from, cannot be dismissed as negativity, requires no alternative, and often resolves the matter outright by surfacing that the parties held different unstated numbers."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are the questions I did not ask in meetings this week. Help me work out which were disagreements in disguise and how to raise them now."
},
{
  id: "noticing-agreement-too-fast",
  track: "noticing", level: "costly",
  title: "Agreement reached faster than the problem is hard is a report on the room, not on the problem",
  source: "Sunstein and Hastie, Wiser: Getting Beyond Groupthink to Make Groups Smarter (2015)",
  gateIntro: "A decision that has divided the team for two months was resolved in eleven minutes this morning. The resolution came after the head of product stated a preference early and clearly. Everyone in the room agreed. Walking out, you cannot reconstruct what changed, and neither can the person you ask in the corridor, who says something about it being good to finally have a direction.",
  evidence: "Sunstein and Hastie synthesise decades of work on group deliberation, including studies showing that groups tend to amplify rather than correct individual errors, that shared information dominates discussion over information held by only one member, and that deliberation frequently increases confidence without increasing accuracy. The underlying findings on the shared-information bias and on group polarisation are well replicated. The specific prescription that fast agreement should be treated as a warning is the authors' inference from them.",
  replication: "replicated",
  idea: "Compare how long the agreement took with how hard the problem was, and treat a large mismatch as evidence that something other than reasoning closed it.",
  why: "The useful property of this test is that it needs no domain knowledge and no view on the merits. You are not asking whether the decision is right. You are comparing two observable quantities, the duration of the deliberation and the historical difficulty of the question, and noticing when they are wildly out of proportion. That comparison is available to the most junior person in the room, which is exactly the property you want in a detector, because the people best placed to see the mismatch are frequently the people least placed to argue the substance.\n\nWhat usually closed it is one of a short list: a senior preference stated early, exhaustion, or a deadline. None of those is illegitimate. A decision does have to get made, and a senior person is entitled to make it. The problem is when the room subsequently remembers a consensus rather than a call, because a remembered consensus cannot be revisited when the assumptions change, and an acknowledged call can.",
  failureMode: "The eleven minute agreement gets recorded in the notes as team aligned on approach. Four months later, when the approach is in trouble, there is no record of a decision-maker and no record of the open questions, so the review finds a collective failure where there was in fact one call and eight people who did not object.",
  worked: "Do not attack the decision, which may well be correct. Attack the label on it, which is the part that will cause the damage:\n\n\"Can I check something about process rather than substance. We have been going round on this for two months and closed it in about ten minutes after Priya said what she preferred. I am fine with the outcome. I want it written down as Priya's call with the open questions listed, not as team consensus, because in four months the difference will matter a lot.\"\n\nThat is a request almost nobody refuses, and it is more valuable than winning the argument would have been. A decision with an owner and a list of open questions is revisitable. A consensus is not.",
  experiment: "Go back through your last five significant decisions and put two numbers beside each: roughly how long the question had been open, and how long the meeting that closed it took. Then check what the notes called the outcome. Look for the pattern where the shortest closures are the ones recorded as consensus.",
  reflection: "Which recent decision closed far faster than its difficulty warranted, and is it recorded as somebody's call or as agreement?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is the ratio of deliberation time to problem difficulty a useful signal, and what should you ask for when the ratio is badly off?",
    a: "Because it requires no domain expertise and no view on the merits, so the most junior person present can apply it, and they are often best placed to see it. When the ratio is off, ask for the outcome to be recorded as a named person's call with the open questions listed rather than as consensus, since a call can be revisited when assumptions change and a remembered consensus cannot."
  },
  deepDive: "A long-running decision on my team closed very fast after a senior preference was stated. Help me write the request to have it recorded as a call rather than as consensus."
},
{
  id: "noticing-cost-curve-of-raising-it",
  track: "noticing", level: "costly",
  title: "The cost of raising it rises faster than the cost of being wrong about it",
  source: "Barry Boehm's cost-of-change curve, and the general observation in design review practice that objections get more expensive to make as commitment accumulates",
  gateIntro: "You have a doubt about the data model. Today it is a paragraph in a design doc that four people have read. In six weeks it will be a schema in production with three services reading from it and a migration nobody has budgeted for. Right now you are about seventy per cent sure your doubt is wrong, and that seventy per cent is the reason you have not written the comment.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the comment as you would leave it on the doc today, at seventy per cent sure you are wrong.",
  evidence: "Boehm's original data, from a small number of large software projects in the 1970s and 1980s, showed defect repair costs rising steeply with the phase at which the defect was found. The specific multipliers have been challenged repeatedly and do not hold uniformly under modern practice. What survives the criticism is the direction and the rough shape of the curve, which is all this entry rests on. Treat any particular ratio you have heard quoted as unsupported.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Your confidence threshold for speaking should fall as the cost of the intervention falls, so a doubt worth nothing at the point of a doc comment is still worth raising even when you expect to be wrong.",
  why: "People apply one threshold to speaking, usually around whether they are more likely right than not, and hold it constant while the cost of the intervention changes by orders of magnitude. That is the error. A comment on a doc costs a minute of the author's attention and can be answered in a line. The same objection raised after the schema is in production costs a migration, a coordination meeting across three teams, and a reputational hit for the person who designed it. If the cost of raising has moved by a factor of a hundred, the confidence you should demand of yourself before raising it has to move too.\n\nThe implication runs the other way as well, and it is the less comfortable half. Late in the process the threshold should be genuinely high, and an objection you would have raised for free in week one is not automatically worth raising in week nine. Applying a flat threshold makes you both too quiet early and too loud late, which is the worst available combination.",
  failureMode: "You wait to be sure. By the time you are sure, the model has three consumers and the person who designed it has defended it twice in public. Your now well-founded objection arrives at the exact moment it is most expensive to act on and most costly for its author to accept, and it is rejected on grounds that have nothing to do with whether you are right.",
  worked: "The low-confidence early comment needs its confidence stated, otherwise it reads as an assertion and gets defended against:\n\n\"Low confidence, raising it now because it is cheap now. I think the tenant id on the events table wants to be on the parent rather than the row, and I am probably missing why it is here. If I am wrong this costs you one line to tell me. If I am right and we find out in eight weeks it costs a migration.\"\n\nThe closing pair of sentences is the whole move. You have made the asymmetry explicit, which converts your uncertainty from a reason to dismiss you into the reason to answer you now.",
  experiment: "Look at the objections you raised in the last quarter and mark when each was raised relative to when the work committed. Then mark your confidence at the time. If your confidence was roughly constant across early and late interventions, your threshold is flat and you are systematically leaving cheap objections unraised.",
  reflection: "What doubt are you sitting on because you are not sure enough, and what will raising it cost in six weeks compared with today?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why should your confidence threshold for raising an objection change over the life of a project, and in which direction?",
    a: "Because the cost of the intervention changes by orders of magnitude while most people hold a single threshold constant. Early, when a comment costs a minute, the threshold should be low enough that you raise doubts you expect to be wrong. Late, when it costs a migration and a public retreat, it should be genuinely high. A flat threshold makes you too quiet early and too loud late."
  },
  deepDive: "I have a low-confidence doubt about a design that is still at the doc stage. Help me write it as a comment that makes the cost asymmetry explicit."
},
{
  id: "noticing-whose-call-is-it",
  track: "noticing", level: "everyday",
  title: "Half of what feels like disagreement is a decision that was never yours to make",
  source: "The distinction between a decision right and an input right, as formalised in RAPID and similar decision-rights frameworks",
  gateIntro: "The design team has changed the empty state illustration on a screen your service powers. You think the new one is worse and communicates less. You have said so twice, once in the review and once in a message, and both times the answer was a polite thank you and no change. You are drafting a third message. Nobody has asked you to stop, and nobody has agreed with you either.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the last thing you are going to say about this, in a form that closes it rather than continuing it.",
  evidence: "No trial. Decision-rights frameworks are consulting instruments, adopted widely because they resolve a recurring organisational confusion, and evaluated almost entirely by testimonial. There is no body of evidence that teams using them make better decisions. The distinction between having input and having the call is nonetheless a real structural fact about how organisations allocate authority, whether or not anybody has written it down.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Establish whether you hold the decision or merely an input before you spend anything on the disagreement, because an input is discharged by being stated clearly once and is not owed agreement.",
  why: "Most persistent low-grade friction in organisations is this confusion rather than genuine conflict. You gave your input, it was heard, and the person with the call decided differently. Nothing has gone wrong. What makes it feel wrong is an unexamined assumption that being heard means being agreed with, and that assumption keeps generating a third message, a fourth conversation, and eventually a reputation.\n\nThe distinction also protects you in the other direction, which is the part people forget. If you do hold the call, then deferring to a louder voice is not being collaborative, it is failing to do your job, and the person who will be asked about it in six months is you. So the question is worth asking explicitly rather than assumed, and it has a plain answer that somebody can usually give you in one sentence.",
  failureMode: "You send the third message. The design lead now has a relationship problem to manage as well as a decision to make, and the next time your service has a genuine constraint on their work you are the person who litigates illustrations. The cost lands on the objection after this one.",
  worked: "The closing move is to state the position once for the record and then explicitly hand the decision back:\n\n\"Last thing from me on this, and then it is yours. I think the new empty state loses the explanation of why the list is empty, and I expect that to show up as support tickets. That is my input and I have now given it twice, so I will stop. Your call, and I am not going to relitigate it.\"\n\nSaying I will stop out loud is what makes this work rather than just being quiet. It converts a fading argument into a completed one, and it means that if the support tickets do arrive, your position is on the record without you ever having had to be right at anybody.",
  experiment: "List the five things you are currently pushing on. For each, write down who has the call, in one name. Where you cannot name a person, that is the actual problem and it is a better thing to raise than the substance. Where you can name someone and it is not you, check how many times you have made your point.",
  reflection: "Which argument are you currently continuing after your input has already been heard and decided against?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is the difference between holding a decision right and holding an input right, and how is an input discharged?",
    a: "The call is the authority to decide; an input is the right to be heard before someone else decides. An input is discharged by being stated clearly once, or at most twice, and it is not owed agreement. Continuing past that point converts a decision disagreement into a relationship problem and spends credibility that the next objection will need."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are five things I am currently pushing on at work. Help me identify who actually holds the call on each, and which ones I should stop arguing."
},
{
  id: "noticing-second-occurrence",
  track: "noticing", level: "everyday",
  title: "One instance is an anecdote you cannot act on, and two is enough to raise",
  source: "Standard practice in incident and quality review, where a repeat is treated as a different class of event from a first occurrence",
  gateIntro: "In March a deploy went out that had not been reviewed, and it was explained as a one-off caused by an on-call handover. Yesterday the same thing happened, with a different person and a different explanation. Both times the individual account was entirely reasonable. You are aware you have now noticed something twice, and you are also aware that raising it will sound like you are keeping a file on people.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence that raises the pattern without making it about either of the two people involved.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is convention rather than finding: quality and incident processes across many industries treat recurrence as the trigger for systemic investigation, on the reasoning that a single event is consistent with too many explanations to discriminate between them. The threshold of two is arbitrary and chosen for tractability, not derived from any analysis of base rates.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Hold a single anomaly and raise the second, because one occurrence is compatible with too many explanations to argue about, and two with different people is a claim about the system that nobody has to defend personally.",
  why: "The first occurrence is genuinely weak evidence, and raising it puts you in an argument you will lose on the merits. Every individual explanation offered for a one-off is usually true. There really was a handover, it really was unusual, and the person really would not normally do that. You have no counter to any of it, because you are arguing about a sample of one and the local explanation always fits a sample of one.\n\nThe second occurrence changes the object under discussion. Two events, different people, different local explanations, same outcome is a statement about the conditions rather than the individuals, and it is much harder to explain away because the two local explanations now have to account for why they produced the same result. It is also far safer to raise, since a claim about conditions does not require you to have kept a file on anybody. The cost of waiting is real: something can happen twice before you speak. That cost is usually worth paying for the enormous gain in the strength and safety of the claim.",
  failureMode: "You raise the first one. It is explained, plausibly, and you accept the explanation because it is plausible. The explanation is now on the record as having addressed the issue, so when it happens a second time the framing is already set: this was addressed in March, and you are the person who is still on about it.",
  worked: "Raise the conditions, keep both individuals out of the sentence, and make the point about the explanations rather than the events:\n\n\"We have now had two unreviewed deploys, in March and yesterday, different people, and both had a reasonable individual explanation. I do not think either person did anything wrong. What I take from having two different reasonable explanations produce the same outcome is that the review step is easy to bypass under time pressure, and I think that is worth twenty minutes rather than another explanation.\"\n\nThe sentence that protects you is I do not think either person did anything wrong, and it needs to arrive before the ask rather than after it.",
  experiment: "Start a private list with two columns, the anomaly and the date, and add to it without acting. Give nothing on the list a second thought until an entry gets a second date. When one does, write the pattern claim before you write anything about either instance, and check that the claim survives with both names removed.",
  reflection: "What have you now seen twice, with different people involved, and have you raised it as a pattern or as two separate incidents?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is the second occurrence a much stronger thing to raise than the first, and what is the cost of the rule?",
    a: "Because a single event is compatible with a local explanation that always fits and that you cannot argue against, whereas two events with different people and different local explanations force the question of why both produced the same outcome, which makes the claim about conditions rather than individuals and much safer to raise. The cost is real: something happens twice before you speak."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are anomalies I have noticed once each on my team. Help me decide which are worth logging and watching versus raising now."
},
{
  id: "noticing-state-degrades-detection",
  track: "noticing", level: "costly",
  title: "Tired, late and outnumbered is when you notice least and defer most",
  source: "The fatigue and vigilance literature underpinning duty-time limits in aviation, summarised in Dinges and colleagues' dose-response work on sleep restriction",
  gateIntro: "It is the fourth hour of an incident bridge. You have been on since six. Someone proposes a mitigation that involves flushing a cache you are fairly sure holds the only copy of some in-flight state, and you cannot immediately reconstruct why you think that. Four people are waiting, the incident commander is asking for objections, and you are aware that you are not at your sharpest.",
  gatePrompt: "You are too tired to be sure and the room is waiting. Write the sentence that stops the action without requiring you to win an argument.",
  evidence: "Controlled laboratory studies of chronic sleep restriction show cumulative, dose-dependent degradation in sustained attention and reaction time, with a striking dissociation: subjective ratings of sleepiness plateau while objective performance keeps declining. That dissociation is well replicated and is the basis for prescriptive duty limits in aviation and rail. The extension to noticing and voicing a disagreement specifically has not been measured, and is an inference from the vigilance findings.",
  replication: "replicated",
  idea: "When your state is degraded, switch from arguing the substance to asking for the action to be delayed, because delay is a request you can win in a condition where the argument is one you cannot.",
  why: "The dangerous finding in the sleep literature is not that tired people perform worse. It is that they do not know how much worse, because the subjective signal saturates while the objective decline continues. Applied to a bridge call at hour four, this means your own sense of being basically fine is uninformative, and so is everyone else's. This is why duty limits are prescriptive rather than discretionary: the judgement that would decide whether to keep going is the judgement that is impaired.\n\nThe move follows from what you can and cannot do in that state. You cannot reliably reconstruct the argument for why the cache matters, and attempting to will lose, because a half-remembered objection is easy to dismiss and you will accept the dismissal. What you can do is state that you have an unretrieved objection and ask for the time to retrieve it. That converts the question from is he right, which you cannot currently establish, into can this wait ten minutes, which usually it can, and which nobody has to concede anything to grant.",
  failureMode: "You try to argue it, get half the argument out, and it is answered with something reasonable that you cannot immediately counter because you are too tired to counter anything. You defer. The cache is flushed. Two hours later you remember exactly why it mattered, at which point the in-flight state is gone and the incident has a second phase.",
  worked: "Ask for time, not for agreement, and put your state on the record because it is a legitimate operational fact:\n\n\"I want to stop this one for ten minutes. I have a strong feeling that cache holds in-flight state we cannot rebuild, and I cannot reconstruct the reasoning right now. I have been on since six and I am not going to argue it well. If I am wrong we lose ten minutes. If I am right and we flush it, we cannot undo it.\"\n\nNaming the asymmetry is what makes ten minutes obviously cheap. Naming your own fatigue is what stops your inability to argue being read as the absence of a reason.",
  experiment: "Look back at the last three incidents that ran past four hours. For each, find a decision made in the back half and ask who objected. If objections are concentrated in the first hour, that is not because the back half was easier. It is the vigilance decrement showing up in your incident record, and it argues for a handover rule rather than for individual willpower.",
  reflection: "In your last long incident, what did you defer to that you would have questioned at hour one?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is the dissociation in the sleep restriction findings, and what does it imply about how you should intervene when you are degraded?",
    a: "Subjective sleepiness plateaus while objective performance keeps declining, so your own sense of being fine carries no information about how impaired you are. It implies you should not try to win the substantive argument, which you will lose from a half-retrieved position. Instead ask for a delay, state your fatigue as an operational fact, and name the asymmetry between the cost of waiting and the cost of an irreversible action."
  },
  deepDive: "Help me design a rule for my team about who is allowed to make irreversible calls late in a long incident, and what the handover trigger should be."
},
{
  id: "noticing-absence-of-dissent-is-data",
  track: "noticing", level: "line",
  title: "A meeting with no objections has told you about the meeting, not about the plan",
  source: "Amy Edmondson, on psychological safety and the interpretation of error and objection rates (Psychological Safety and Learning Behavior in Work Teams, 1999)",
  gateIntro: "You now run four teams. In the weekly review, three of them raise problems and argue with your framing. The fourth presents cleanly every week, hits its dates, and has never disagreed with you in the meeting in five months. Their last two projects both slipped in the final fortnight with issues that turned out to have been known internally for weeks. You have their review tomorrow.",
  gatePrompt: "You are the senior person and their silence is the problem. Write the opening sentence of that review.",
  evidence: "Edmondson's study of hospital nursing units found that the units with the best-rated leadership and team relationships reported substantially more medication errors, not fewer. Independent observation established that the higher-reporting units were not making more errors, they were surfacing them. The inversion has held up as a caution about reading reported-problem rates, and the underlying psychological safety construct has been extensively studied since, though effect sizes vary considerably across settings and much of the downstream work is cross-sectional and self-reported.",
  replication: "replicated",
  idea: "Read objection rate as a measure of whether objecting is safe rather than as a measure of whether there is anything to object to, and treat the silent team as the one needing attention rather than the one performing well.",
  why: "Edmondson went looking for a relationship between team quality and error rates and found it pointing the wrong way. The better units reported more errors. The resolution was that reporting and occurrence are different variables and only one of them was being measured. That result is the reason a clean meeting cannot be read at face value: you are observing what reached you, which is a function of both what happened and what people were willing to say, and you have no way to separate the two from inside the meeting.\n\nFor anyone senior this inverts the natural reading. The team that argues with you is giving you a live feed. The team that never does is giving you a filtered one, and the filter's coefficient is unknown to you and known to them. The slipping projects are the confirmation: the information existed, it was held internally for weeks, and the channel that should have carried it did not. That is not a competence problem in the team. It is a report on what the team believes it costs to bring you a problem early.",
  failureMode: "You praise the quiet team publicly for how smoothly their reviews run and hold them up as the model. Every other team now updates on what you reward, and the objection rate across all four falls. You have converted a measurement problem into an organisational one, and your visibility into all four teams is now equally poor.",
  worked: "Do not ask why they never disagree, which invites a reassuring answer. Ask for something specific that has to have existed:\n\n\"I want to change how we run these. The last two projects both slipped late on things the team knew about weeks earlier, and I do not think that is a competence problem, I think it is that nothing bad reaches me until it is certain. So for tomorrow I want the opposite of a clean review. Bring me the thing you are least sure about and most expect me to push back on, and I will tell you what I do with it.\"\n\nThen the hard part, which is the entire point: whatever they bring, your response is the experiment. If the first genuinely uncomfortable thing they raise gets challenged, the channel closes for another five months.",
  experiment: "Count objections per meeting per team for a month, as a plain tally with no judgement attached. Then set the counts beside each team's rate of late surprises. If the quiet teams are the ones with late surprises, you have measured your own filter, and the intervention belongs to you rather than to them.",
  reflection: "Which of your teams or peers never disagrees with you, and what has arrived from them late that they must have known early?",
  recall: {
    q: "What did Edmondson's nursing unit study find, and how should a senior person read a team that never objects?",
    a: "The units with the best leadership and team relationships reported more medication errors, and independent observation showed they were surfacing them rather than making more. So reported-problem rate measures safety of reporting as much as underlying quality. A team that never objects is reporting on what it believes objecting costs, and late surprises are the confirmation that the channel is filtered."
  },
  deepDive: "Help me design a month of measurement across my teams that would tell me whether the quiet ones are safe or just quiet."
}
);
